<Section title="Geometric Properties" id="geometric_properties"></Section>

<SubSection title="Cross Sectional Area" id="cross-section-area">
Cross sectional area is the area of the face created by slicing through an object or geometry. The dimensions are <InlineEquation equation = "length^2"/>, with some common units being <InlineEquation equation = "mm^2"/>  or <InlineEquation equation = "in^2"/>.
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="First Moment of Area: Centroid of an Area" id="first-moi">
The first moment of the area A with respect to the z-axis is given by <InlineEquation equation='Q_x = \\int_A y dA = \\Sigma yA' /> .

 The first moment of the area A with respect to the y-axis is given by <InlineEquation equation='Q_y = \\int_A x dA  = \\Sigma zA' />.



 <InlineEquation equation='(\\overline{x}, \\overline{y})' title="The centroid of an area." background="True"/>

<Image src='/sol/bending/Centroid.png' width='2'> Centroid of a body </Image>

<DisplayEquation equation='\\bar{x} = \\frac{1}{A}\\int_A x dA\\' title="x coordinate." background="True"/>
<DisplayEquation equation='\\bar{y} = \\frac{1}{A}\\int_A y dA\\' title="y coordinate." background="True"/> 

Complex (or composite) areas can be divided into smaller, easier parts.

<Image src='/sol/bending/CompositeCentroid.png' width='6'>  Centroid of a composite body</Image>

<DisplayEquation equation="\\bar{X} = \\frac{1}{A_{tot}}\\sum_i (A_i\\bar{y}_i)\\" title="Composite x coordinate" background="True"/>
<DisplayEquation equation='\\bar{Y} = \\frac{1}{A_{tot}} \\sum_i (A_i\\bar{y}_i)' title="Composite y coordinate" background="True"/>
</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Second Moment or Area Moment of Inertia" id="second-moi">
The moment of inertia of the area <InlineEquation equation='A' /> with respect to the x-axis.

<DisplayEquation equation='I_x = \\int_A y^2 dA' title="x-axis second moment." background="True" />

The moment of inertia of the area <InlineEquation equation='A' /> with respect to the y-axis.

<DisplayEquation equation='I_y = \\int_A x^2 dA' title="y-axis second moment." background="True" />

<br>
<em>Note:</em>  polar moment of inertia in this plane 

<DisplayEquation equation='J = \\int_A \\rho^2 dA = \\int_A (y^2 + x^2)dA = I_y + I_x\\' title="Polar moment of Inertia." background="True"/>

 <strong>Parallel-axis theorem:</strong> the moment of inertia about an axis through C parallel to the axis through the centroid C is related to <InlineEquation equation='I_C' />. 
 <DisplayEquation equation="I_C = I_{C'} +A'd_{CC'}^2\\" title="Parallel axis theorem." background="True"/>

</SubSection >

<SubSection title="Polar Moment of Inertia" id="polar_moment_of_inertia">

<DisplayEquation equation='J = \\frac{\\pi R^4}{2} = \\frac{\\pi D^4}{32}' title="Solid shaft (radius and diameter)." background="True" derivation="True" id="tor-pmi">
    

<DisplayEquation equation=`\\begin{aligned}J &= \\int_A{\\rho^2dA}\\\\
&= \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}{\\int_0^R{\\rho^2(\\rho \\; d\\rho \\; d\\theta)}}\\\\
&= \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}{d\\theta}\\int_0^R{\\rho^3d\\rho}\\\\
&= 2\\pi\\left[\\frac{1}{4} \\rho^4\\right]_0^R\\\\
&= \\frac{\\pi R^4}{2} = \\frac{\\pi D^4}{32} \\end{aligned}` />

</DisplayEquation>

<DisplayEquation equation="J = \\frac{\\pi}{2}(R_o^4-R_i^4) = \\frac{\\pi}{32}(D_o^4-D_i^4)\\" title="Hollow shaft (inner and outer radius)." background="True" derivation="True" id="tor-hol">
    

<DisplayEquation equation=`\\begin{aligned}J &= \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}{\\int_{R_i}^{R_o}{\\rho^3 \\; d\\rho \\; d\\theta}} \\\\
&= \\frac{\\pi}{2} [{R_o}^4 - {R_i}^4]\\end{aligned}` />
  
</DisplayEquation>

</SubSection>

<SubSection title="Summary: Moment of Inertia" id="summary-moi">

    <center><em>Common shapes about the origin: <InlineEquation equation='I'/> and <InlineEquation equation='J_O'/></em></center>
    <center><em>Common shapes about the centroid: <InlineEquation equation='\\bar{I}'/> and <InlineEquation equation='J_c'/></em></center>
